<section style="padding:72px 24px 40px;"><div style="max-width:860px;margin:0 auto;"><div class="rich"><p>Load bearing wall removal in Perth nearly always starts with a brick house. WA is the only state where more than half of detached houses are brick, at 68 per cent, and in a double-brick home the inner leaf of the external walls and some internal walls usually carry the ceiling, the roof or an upper floor. So the wall between your kitchen and lounge may well be holding something up. Find out before anyone picks up a hammer.</p><p>Kings Park Structural Co is advertising, not an engineering firm. We pass your enquiry to the independent structural engineering firm that advertises here, and it quotes you and contracts with you directly. The engineer who takes your job works out what the wall carries, sizes the beam and its supports, and provides the drawing and certificate your building surveyor needs. A registered builder does the building work.</p></div></div></section>
<section style="padding:0 24px 64px;"><div style="max-width:1100px;margin:0 auto;">
    <span class="kicker">What We Handle</span>
    <h2 class="section-heading" style="font-size:clamp(22px,3vw,32px);margin:0 0 28px;">What's Included in Every Visit</h2>
    <div class="feat-grid" style="display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(3,1fr);gap:20px;">
      <div style="background:#fff;border:1px solid #ECE5D8;border-radius:10px;padding:26px;box-shadow:0 2px 8px rgba(14,77,110,0.05);">
        <h3 style="font-family:'Figtree',sans-serif;font-size:18px;font-weight:800;color:#15191E;margin:0 0 8px;">Load path check</h3>
        <p style="font-size:14.5px;line-height:1.7;color:#555;margin:0;">The engineer traces what sits on the wall (roof framing, ceiling, an upper floor or another wall) and where that load has to go once the wall is gone.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="background:#fff;border:1px solid #ECE5D8;border-radius:10px;padding:26px;box-shadow:0 2px 8px rgba(14,77,110,0.05);">
        <h3 style="font-family:'Figtree',sans-serif;font-size:18px;font-weight:800;color:#15191E;margin:0 0 8px;">Steel or LVL beam sizing</h3>
        <p style="font-size:14.5px;line-height:1.7;color:#555;margin:0;">The beam is sized for your span and your loads, with bearing lengths and connections specified so the builder isn't guessing on site.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="background:#fff;border:1px solid #ECE5D8;border-radius:10px;padding:26px;box-shadow:0 2px 8px rgba(14,77,110,0.05);">
        <h3 style="font-family:'Figtree',sans-serif;font-size:18px;font-weight:800;color:#15191E;margin:0 0 8px;">Posts, point loads and pad footings</h3>
        <p style="font-size:14.5px;line-height:1.7;color:#555;margin:0;">A beam dumps its load at each end. The design covers the posts or brick piers there and any pad footing needed underneath.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="background:#fff;border:1px solid #ECE5D8;border-radius:10px;padding:26px;box-shadow:0 2px 8px rgba(14,77,110,0.05);">
        <h3 style="font-family:'Figtree',sans-serif;font-size:18px;font-weight:800;color:#15191E;margin:0 0 8px;">Temporary propping</h3>
        <p style="font-size:14.5px;line-height:1.7;color:#555;margin:0;">The roof and ceiling have to be held up while the wall comes out. Ask the engineer to cover propping in the documents so the builder works to a plan.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="background:#fff;border:1px solid #ECE5D8;border-radius:10px;padding:26px;box-shadow:0 2px 8px rgba(14,77,110,0.05);">
        <h3 style="font-family:'Figtree',sans-serif;font-size:18px;font-weight:800;color:#15191E;margin:0 0 8px;">Drawing and certificate for the permit</h3>
        <p style="font-size:14.5px;line-height:1.7;color:#555;margin:0;">You get a beam specification or drawing and a signed certificate that your building surveyor can rely on when the permit application goes in.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="background:#fff;border:1px solid #ECE5D8;border-radius:10px;padding:26px;box-shadow:0 2px 8px rgba(14,77,110,0.05);">
        <h3 style="font-family:'Figtree',sans-serif;font-size:18px;font-weight:800;color:#15191E;margin:0 0 8px;">Clear split of who does what</h3>
        <p style="font-size:14.5px;line-height:1.7;color:#555;margin:0;">The engineer assesses and designs. A registered builder removes the wall and installs the beam, and the permit comes from the council or a building surveyor.</p>
      </div>
</div>
